WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.120
Where does river water go when it enters the ocean? Does it spread out everywhere? Does it do a loop-de-loop?

00:00:06.220 --> 00:00:11.000
Does it flow along the coast? It sounds like it should be easy, but it's anything but.

00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:13.670
It's a research problem, I did it for my PhD,

00:00:13.809 --> 00:00:16.300
river water flowing down a river,

00:00:16.300 --> 00:00:21.100
hits the mouth and it enters the ocean. But where does it actually go? We don't really know.

00:00:21.100 --> 00:00:24.660
(Brady: I always thought it just, like, would blast out to sea like in a...)

00:00:25.570 --> 00:00:27.200
(slowing plume.)

00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:29.200
The Coriolis force is zero on the equator,

00:00:29.380 --> 00:00:33.500
so the river Amazon does just blast out in a plume like you described.

00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:37.200
But then when we move into the Northern hemisphere and the Southern hemisphere

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:42.419
the Earth's rotation creates this Coriolis force, which is super, super important.

00:00:42.579 --> 00:00:47.600
So that is going to cause your river in the Northern hemisphere to turn to the right.

00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:53.000
And you have to imagine it, when I say 'right,' you have to imagine travelling down the river and as you reach the coast turn right.

00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:57.040
So that could be east or west depending on where your river's flowing. And then the southern hemisphere,

00:00:57.080 --> 00:00:59.740
same thing, but you're gonna turn to the left.

00:01:00.180 --> 00:01:05.800
And we can see this in satellite images. You get this by doing experiments, that's what I did: did some experiments in the lab.

00:01:05.800 --> 00:01:09.200
You know, went clockwise/anti-clockwise you see right and left.

00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:13.400
Did it without rotation and it looks like the Amazon in that big plume.

00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:19.200
Ultimately, the Earth's rotation is the most important factor in determining where this river water goes.

00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:21.500
Because the effect of the Coriolis force,

00:01:21.740 --> 00:01:26.820
it actually scales exactly as sine of latitude, as in the sine function.

00:01:26.820 --> 00:01:27.900
So as you move further away

00:01:27.900 --> 00:01:31.589
it gets bigger and bigger and bigger and as you move further south it gets more and more negative.

00:01:31.590 --> 00:01:37.320
We want to model a river entering the ocean. So here's our river. This is our coast. We're gonna be in the northern hemisphere,

00:01:37.570 --> 00:01:41.939
being currently in the UK. River water comes in and then it's going to turn to the right.

00:01:41.939 --> 00:01:47.969
This is what the Coriolis force does. So the water's coming in. It's turning to the side and it's flowing along the coast.

00:01:47.970 --> 00:01:51.239
So what you actually get are two very different features.

00:01:51.240 --> 00:01:57.329
You see these on satellite images, or you can do experiments - you can actually see these two things forming.

00:01:57.329 --> 00:02:00.329
So there's this sort of large whirlpool that forms near the river mouth,

00:02:00.759 --> 00:02:05.759
but there's this particular point here where it divides. So you're a particle coming in,

00:02:05.759 --> 00:02:06.310
you can either

00:02:06.310 --> 00:02:11.670
go like this for a while, or sort of stay there, or if you're near enough the edge you sort of go around

00:02:12.180 --> 00:02:16.079
and then you enter this current. Now, the bit near the source

00:02:16.239 --> 00:02:21.479
is interesting from a maths viewpoint, but it's not super big in terms of trying to understand this problem.

00:02:21.480 --> 00:02:24.480
You just have a big clump of river water near your river mouth,

00:02:24.879 --> 00:02:31.468
we would expect that. Perhaps. But then going along the coast what we want to know is, well, how far

00:02:31.860 --> 00:02:34.460
along the coast does this river water go?

00:02:34.460 --> 00:02:37.100
How deep is this river water? And

00:02:37.450 --> 00:02:43.469
maybe how far away from the coast is it? Most of the pollution in our oceans comes from rivers.

00:02:43.870 --> 00:02:46.200
So if we're going to start cleaning up our oceans

00:02:46.540 --> 00:02:52.200
the sensible thing is to start where there is the most pollution, and this could of course

00:02:52.200 --> 00:02:58.079
you know be either tiny plastic beads or chemical pollution that you can't see - so it's not a case of there's a giant blob of

00:02:58.209 --> 00:02:59.469
garbage, let's clean it up.

00:02:59.469 --> 00:03:03.239
You need to think about, you need to model this. You need to think about the physics, the maths of it and

00:03:03.310 --> 00:03:07.469
you need to be clever. So what we want to know is, we want to know its width,

00:03:07.599 --> 00:03:10.858
so I'm going to call 'w' naught. We want to know how fast it's going,

00:03:11.169 --> 00:03:16.109
velocity, 'u' naught, and we want to know how deep it is and I'm gonna call that h naught. So if we're looking at it

00:03:16.109 --> 00:03:21.418
from the side on you've got your river surface with the waves and you've got your sort of current here

00:03:21.419 --> 00:03:26.369
which is going to be your fresh water, your river. And then below it here, you've got your sea, your saltwater.

00:03:26.650 --> 00:03:30.030
So this is the view from above we want to know the width of it, how far off the coast,

00:03:30.280 --> 00:03:32.229
wanna know how fast it's moving, and

00:03:32.229 --> 00:03:36.179
then if we sort of flip it on its side, we want to know how far down in the ocean

00:03:36.430 --> 00:03:42.540
does that current go? Because if we know these things, you know, we clean up that amount of distance from the coast.

00:03:42.540 --> 00:03:47.280
We, we clean up the top layer of the ocean. It's incredibly important information. We're actually

00:03:47.980 --> 00:03:52.880
you know, gonna clean up our oceans, fixing pollution and the maths is also awesome, which is an added bonus.

00:03:52.900 --> 00:04:00.060
We're doing fluid mechanics. So the key thing really is to think about, what are the variables?

00:04:00.300 --> 00:04:03.140
What are the parameters that are actually going to affect

00:04:03.280 --> 00:04:05.420
this particular situation.

00:04:06.640 --> 00:04:11.700
So first of all, I've hopefully convinced you that the Earth's rotation is

00:04:11.980 --> 00:04:18.239
incredibly important. If we're on the equator with the Amazon, it behaves very differently in the northern hemisphere, the southern hemisphere.

00:04:18.240 --> 00:04:23.440
So we're going to have our Coriolis parameter. Our rotation rate is going to be f.

00:04:23.580 --> 00:04:29.400
It's going to play such a major role that we're also to assume that this guy, the rotation, dominates.

00:04:29.400 --> 00:04:35.760
What else might affect the movement of this water? Perhaps how much water is leaving the river?

00:04:36.070 --> 00:04:41.640
So what is the the volume flux, or the outflow rate from our river? So I'm going to call that one Q.

00:04:42.360 --> 00:04:45.560
Q is our volume flux.

00:04:45.560 --> 00:04:51.660
So I've got a giant river like the Amazon compared to a tiny little stream, different things will happen. And then it turns out

00:04:51.660 --> 00:04:55.830
the third and final thing that we're going to need is going to be the density difference.

00:04:56.100 --> 00:05:02.020
River water is going to float on top of the salt water. That's why we want to know the depth of that top layer.

00:05:02.360 --> 00:05:09.840
So we're going to model that with what we call the reduced gravity, g prime, but for our purposes, that's just the density difference.

00:05:09.900 --> 00:05:17.440
It's just a formula which involves the density of the river water, the density of the salt water (the ocean), and gravity.

00:05:17.440 --> 00:05:22.160
So these are our three key parameters and importantly we've said rotation dominates.

00:05:22.160 --> 00:05:25.260
We're then going to have to make some more assumptions.

00:05:25.380 --> 00:05:27.900
So fluid mechanics means

00:05:28.090 --> 00:05:32.189
Navier-Stokes equations. I've talked a lot about Navier-Stokes equations in the past

00:05:32.190 --> 00:05:33.480
so I'm not going to do all of that again.

00:05:33.480 --> 00:05:38.460
But in short, set of equations that model the flow of every fluid. They are super difficult,

00:05:39.130 --> 00:05:44.969
super tricky, we don't really understand them. We can't really solve them. So we're going to make loads and loads of assumptions.

00:05:44.970 --> 00:05:47.190
So, as I said, first assumption is rotation dominates.

00:05:47.260 --> 00:05:51.300
We then also assume that in our particular setup

00:05:51.420 --> 00:05:57.540
the key velocity of our river current is going to be in the direction along the coast.

00:05:57.700 --> 00:06:02.129
There will be some velocity in this boundary current, in this current where, you know,

00:06:02.130 --> 00:06:05.760
it might move a little bit this way, and it might move a little bit down or up,

00:06:05.760 --> 00:06:10.840
but generally it's going along the coast. The rotation is pushing it in that direction.

00:06:10.840 --> 00:06:14.460
So what you're doing by assuming that that velocity is dominant,

00:06:14.460 --> 00:06:21.269
you're reducing the dimensions of the Navier-Stokes. Because as we know, we don't know if solutions exist in 3D.

00:06:21.970 --> 00:06:25.860
So let's not think about 3D. Let's just consider 1D, let's make it easy for ourselves.

00:06:25.860 --> 00:06:29.969
This is how we get around the difficulty of the Navier-Stokes in practice.

00:06:30.220 --> 00:06:36.989
So we assume we have this dominant velocity along the coast. And again, we can look at satellite data,

00:06:36.990 --> 00:06:40.950
we can look at experiments, and this is true. So that's gonna be our second assumption.

00:06:40.960 --> 00:06:44.279
We then assume something called hydrostatic pressure,

00:06:44.280 --> 00:06:49.480
which basically just says that your pressure only matters in the depth of the water.

00:06:49.620 --> 00:06:53.180
We don't need to worry too much about what that means, but it helps us a lot.

00:06:53.180 --> 00:07:01.480
So we have that. And then we're gonna have two final things. The next one is going to be that our ocean is infinitely deep.

00:07:02.220 --> 00:07:04.220
Ridiculous, I know, but

00:07:04.300 --> 00:07:09.980
bear with me because the idea here is, as we will see with our eventual answers,

00:07:09.980 --> 00:07:15.580
the river current- that bit of fresh water on top of the ocean- it's pretty shallow, right?

00:07:15.600 --> 00:07:20.100
So we're talking 10 to 20 metres compared to an ocean that's hundreds of metres deep. So

00:07:20.680 --> 00:07:24.990
it seems, at least to me, hopefully to everybody else, that

00:07:25.420 --> 00:07:31.160
the fact that your ocean is so much deeper than that top 10 metres, it's hundreds of metres down,

00:07:31.160 --> 00:07:37.140
you don't expect the bottom of the ocean to interfere with the top layer of your river water.

00:07:37.180 --> 00:07:42.030
So it may as well be infinitely deep. And by doing that it makes the maths a lot easier

00:07:42.030 --> 00:07:47.730
which is always nice. And then our very final thing which is sort of the key to the whole model,

00:07:47.730 --> 00:07:50.550
is that the potential vorticity is conserved.

00:07:51.280 --> 00:07:59.060
So in short it says that your vorticity is sort of how swirly your - it's like the

00:07:59.200 --> 00:08:04.840
whirlpool-iness, swirliness of your water. It just says that if that's at a particular value, it will stay at that value.

00:08:04.960 --> 00:08:11.800
It's something we do all the time in fluid mechanics. So we assume that the PV, potential vorticity, is conserved.

00:08:11.800 --> 00:08:14.140
We have these three important parameters:

00:08:14.140 --> 00:08:15.300
rotation rate,

00:08:15.300 --> 00:08:21.440
volume flux - the amount of water coming in - and the density difference. We make these five assumptions and as if by magic

00:08:21.440 --> 00:08:23.360
we get these incredibly nice

00:08:23.860 --> 00:08:29.260
formulae for the three things we're interested in. 
(Brady: Are the Navier-Stokes equations what give them to you?)

00:08:29.260 --> 00:08:31.440
Yes, so it starts in Navier-Stokes,

00:08:31.440 --> 00:08:35.380
but we're using the the classic applied mathematician trick of,

00:08:35.380 --> 00:08:37.340
let's just make all of these assumptions and

00:08:37.480 --> 00:08:42.800
worry about whether they were good or bad at the end when we get our result. And what we find,

00:08:42.800 --> 00:08:50.480
we get this really neat formula to say that the maximum depth h naught is equal to 2 times the rotation rate,

00:08:50.770 --> 00:08:52.770
times the volume flux,

00:08:52.780 --> 00:08:55.420
divided by the density difference;

00:08:55.420 --> 00:09:02.080
all to the half. Even though we started with the Navier-Stokes equations, these incredibly complicated

00:09:02.160 --> 00:09:08.520
mathematical things we don't understand. It's amazing, it genuinely is amazing that this pops out from

00:09:08.520 --> 00:09:14.180
such a complicated set of equations. And you can think a little bit about whether this makes sense.

00:09:14.180 --> 00:09:20.300
This is saying that the river current gets deeper when your rotation rate is larger.

00:09:20.300 --> 00:09:23.280
Says the river current gets deeper when your volume flux is larger.

00:09:23.290 --> 00:09:27.909
You have more water leaving your river, you expect that the resulting current to be deeper.

00:09:27.910 --> 00:09:32.230
And then finally it says that it's deeper when your density difference is smaller.

00:09:32.540 --> 00:09:36.320
So the density of the two waters are really close together,

00:09:36.320 --> 00:09:43.020
they mix together really well. Whereas when they're really far apart you've got super salty ocean water, really fresh river water -

00:09:43.029 --> 00:09:48.699
they don't mix. You know, it's like mixing water and oil versus mixing salt water, fresh water. You get that difference.

00:09:48.700 --> 00:09:54.159
So it all makes perfect sense and it came out of the equation and it involves the three things we thought were important.

00:09:54.709 --> 00:09:59.769
(Brady: Tom, not being very familiar with these actual parameters, like what a flux density is and that sort of stuff,)

00:09:59.890 --> 00:10:03.759
(what type of numbers do you normally get from typical Earth-sized rivers?)

00:10:04.060 --> 00:10:12.080
So f is really small. f is one rotation of the Earth in 24 hours. So f is tiny, right, because the Earth's huge,

00:10:12.160 --> 00:10:15.200
so it moves very, very slowly. Q, the volume flux,

00:10:15.520 --> 00:10:20.160
so the River Rhine is the biggest river in the North Sea, one of the ones I studied with this work and that we

00:10:20.170 --> 00:10:22.630
compared and got similar results to satellite data.

00:10:22.760 --> 00:10:29.289
You're looking at like 2,000 metres Q per second. For the density difference if your fresh water is 1000,

00:10:30.040 --> 00:10:36.780
then your ocean water is 1025. Sort of, it's one way of measuring it. So it's slightly,

00:10:36.800 --> 00:10:40.680
slightly more dense, so the g prime value, it tends to be quite small.

00:10:40.690 --> 00:10:44.499
So f is quite small, g prime is quite small, but Q can be quite big.

00:10:45.170 --> 00:10:48.640
(Brady: So how deep does that give us, what kind of depths? )
- Sorry, so for the River Rhine

00:10:48.640 --> 00:10:52.520
this guy comes out at about 10 to 20 metres.

00:10:52.520 --> 00:10:56.980
Because of course it will vary depending on the season. So in the summer

00:10:57.140 --> 00:11:03.640
there's a smaller volume flux, you know, the densities might be more similar. Whereas in the winter really large volume flux.

00:11:03.640 --> 00:11:07.029
Different things can happen in different seasons, but you get a value between 10 and 20 metres,

00:11:07.420 --> 00:11:11.920
you measure it with a ship, you measure it on the satellite. It's about 10 metres deep.

00:11:11.920 --> 00:11:17.440
So it's sort of, it's giving us the right kind of values. So if we're going to clean up

00:11:17.990 --> 00:11:23.289
the North Sea along the coast of the Netherlands, France and Germany, we only need to clean the top 10 metres.

00:11:23.290 --> 00:11:28.209
That's the kind of knowledge you can get from these models. Then we also get the width.

00:11:28.209 --> 00:11:33.120
So the width is important because we want to know how far offshore to clean up.

00:11:33.680 --> 00:11:38.079
Thinking practically, we want to know do we clean up 10-20 kilometres out to sea or do we have to go all the way out

00:11:38.080 --> 00:11:44.470
100 kilometres? And we get a really neat formula for the width. w naught, we've got an 8 this time, g prime Q

00:11:44.750 --> 00:11:50.980
divided by f cubed, all to the 1/4. Like before let's think a little bit does this make sense? If we have a

00:11:51.110 --> 00:11:53.090
larger volume flux

00:11:53.090 --> 00:12:00.399
we expect it to be deeper and we expect it to be wider. There's more water. If we have a larger density difference

00:12:01.070 --> 00:12:03.070
this is telling us that

00:12:03.440 --> 00:12:10.060
it's going to be wider. So we saw that it was shallower and here this is telling us it will be wider.

00:12:10.300 --> 00:12:12.760
Again, if you've got the same amount of water

00:12:13.220 --> 00:12:17.140
but you change the density difference, if this is shallower, it's got to go somewhere

00:12:17.140 --> 00:12:19.689
so it sort of goes wider, becomes further away.

00:12:19.690 --> 00:12:22.660
And then with the rotation, if the rotation is larger, think about

00:12:22.760 --> 00:12:28.840
one of those fairground rides where you're in that sort of circular thing and it spins really fast and you're pushed back against the wall.

00:12:28.840 --> 00:12:31.239
So the faster it goes, the more you're forced outwards

00:12:31.250 --> 00:12:36.160
so you expect it to be less wide, because you're forced more towards the coast the faster

00:12:36.160 --> 00:12:40.180
it's moving. And this guy is gonna spit out- it's around 10 to 20 kilometres?

00:12:40.180 --> 00:12:44.340
So it's much bigger than the top one because f is so small, because f is tiny

00:12:44.340 --> 00:12:46.640
we've got it cubed on the bottom so you get a much bigger number.

00:12:46.640 --> 00:12:50.100
So this is now kilometres, 10 to 20 kilometres for the River Rhine.

00:12:50.420 --> 00:12:54.039
Again, it's around 20 kilometres on the satellites.

00:12:54.040 --> 00:13:00.860
So we know just clean up top 10 metres, 20 kilometres out to sea. And then our final result we can get the velocity.

00:13:01.000 --> 00:13:06.160
So the velocity is going to follow a similar pattern except the numbers this time are ridiculous.

00:13:06.160 --> 00:13:09.819
So we have 3 over 2 to the nine-quarters -

00:13:10.100 --> 00:13:18.000
maybe my favourite number. f, g prime, Q to the 1/4. Again, let's just think about, does this make sense?

00:13:18.040 --> 00:13:22.800
We go faster if the Earth rotates faster, if our experiment rotates faster.

00:13:22.980 --> 00:13:27.580
Okay, that seems sensible. If there's a larger density difference

00:13:27.720 --> 00:13:29.220
they're not going to mix as much.

00:13:29.220 --> 00:13:31.279
So if they're not using their energy to mix

00:13:31.279 --> 00:13:37.759
they're probably going to use that energy to go faster. And then, again, if there's more water it's going to be pushed through faster.

00:13:37.800 --> 00:13:44.280
So they all make complete physical sense. This gives us a value of around one metre per second?

00:13:44.700 --> 00:13:52.500
So not super fast, but again the River Rhine, it's around 0.5 to 1 metres per second as well.

00:13:52.500 --> 00:13:56.839
So all of them are within the ballpark and this kind of knowledge actually then,

00:13:56.839 --> 00:14:03.139
you know, we can use this. We can go out to the River Rhine, go out to the Dutch coast and start

00:14:03.390 --> 00:14:05.479
using this, putting this to practice and cleaning up

00:14:05.910 --> 00:14:12.980
our oceans by just doing some maths, some Navier-Stokes, some assumptions and sort of getting these really neat formula

00:14:12.980 --> 00:14:17.620
explaining what's happening.
(Brady: This plume or this current that goes along the coast,)

00:14:17.660 --> 00:14:23.140
(obviously it's constantly being fed by more and more water as the river just keeps flowing and flowing and flowing.)

00:14:23.340 --> 00:14:28.160
(How long is this plume? How does it end, do the pollutants follow the whole coast of Europe forever and - ?)

00:14:28.709 --> 00:14:29.720
Yeah, great question.

00:14:29.720 --> 00:14:34.070
So the River Rhine plume flows along the Dutch coast and then along

00:14:34.230 --> 00:14:37.940
Germany and then it hits the sort of the corner bit where Denmark then pops up.

00:14:37.940 --> 00:14:42.770
And in that corner is the river Elbe, which is the second largest river in the North Sea.

00:14:42.930 --> 00:14:44.930
So it kind of merges into the

00:14:44.940 --> 00:14:50.869
outflow from the river Elbe and then the river Elbe flows up along the coast of Denmark and then goes into the

00:14:51.000 --> 00:14:53.209
Baltic Sea and then sort of all gets trapped

00:14:53.279 --> 00:14:57.769
in the Baltic Sea. And then you've got all kinds of different things happening in there because it's quite a

00:14:58.320 --> 00:15:00.320
stagnant ocean and it only has one

00:15:00.329 --> 00:15:05.809
inlet in and out and it's quite large so you get weird things going on in the Baltic Sea and,

00:15:06.209 --> 00:15:09.799
entering a different sort of modelling regime, different physics happening there.

